+
+      <section class="card" id="question-type-analysis">
+        <h2>Question Type Composition &amp; Trends</h2>
+        <p>
+          Composition is highly structured. Across all observed years, <strong>MCQs = 15</strong> and <strong>Long = 3</strong> appear every year.
+          The remaining variation is in Short questions (9 in 2015–2016, 18 in 2017–2019, and 15 in 2021–2025).
+          This yields three distinct composition regimes.
+        </p>
+        <div class="chart-wrap" id="chart-share"></div>
+
+        <div class="two" style="margin-top: 12px;">
+          <div>
+            <h3 style="margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">Interpretation for educators</h3>
+            <ul style="margin: 8px 0 0 18px;">
+              <li>Exam practice sets should reliably include <strong>15 MCQs</strong> and <strong>3 long questions</strong> as a stable core.</li>
+              <li>Short-question weight is the main lever that changes total paper volume across years in this dataset.</li>
+              <li>Because MCQs and Long questions are constant, shifts in “difficulty feel” may be driven more by <em>content</em> than by section counts.</li>
+            </ul>
+          </div>
+          <div>
+            <h3 style="margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">Observed shares (examples)</h3>
+            <div class="taglist">
+              <div class="tag"><strong>2015–2016</strong>: MCQs 55.6% • Short 33.3% • Long 11.1%</div>
+              <div class="tag"><strong>2017–2019</strong>: MCQs 41.7% • Short 50.0% • Long 8.3%</div>
