+        <p>
+          This section uses keyword frequency on the <code class="inline">question</code> text (after lowercasing and replacing <code class="inline">$</code> with spaces).
+          Stop-words and common instruction words (e.g., “define”, “describe”) are excluded. Results are “signals” for recurring concepts.
+        </p>
+
+        <div class="two">
+          <div>
+            <h3 style="margin: 10px 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">Top keywords (overall)</h3>
+            <div class="chart-wrap" id="chart-keywords"></div>
+            <p class="small">Top terms are dominated by programming fundamentals and databases: <em>data</em>, <em>database</em>, <em>function</em>, <em>loop</em>, <em>key</em>, <em>table</em>.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div>
+            <h3 style="margin: 10px 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">Frequent two-word phrases</h3>
+            <table aria-label="Top bigrams">
+              <thead>
+                <tr>
+                  <th>Phrase</th>
+                  <th class="num">Count</th>
+                </tr>
+              </thead>
+              <tbody>
+                <tr><td>primary key</td><td class="num">12</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>bytes bytes</td><td class="num">11</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>format specifier</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>foreign key</td><td class="num">9</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>data type</td><td class="num">8</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>switch statement</td><td class="num">7</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>include stdio</td><td class="num">7</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>database model</td><td class="num">7</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>language database</td><td class="num">7</td></tr>
+                <tr><td>while loop</td><td class="num">6</td></tr>
+              </tbody>
+            </table>
+            <p class="small" style="margin-top: 10px;">
+              Many phrases are strongly aligned with DB concepts (keys, relational database) and C programming fundamentals (format specifiers, control statements).
+            </p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <h3 style="margin: 14px 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">Keyword trends over time (top 10 terms)</h3>
+        <div class="chart-wrap" id="chart-kw-trends"></div>
+        <p class="small">
+          Use this chart to see whether emphasis on certain vocabulary rises or falls within the observed years.
+          Note that a missing year (2020) can create a visual “jump” that reflects absent data rather than a true exam change.
+        </p>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="card" id="exam-prep-insights">
+        <h2>Strategic Insights for Exam Preparation</h2>
+        <p>
+          The most actionable patterns in this dataset are (1) stable section counts for MCQs and Long questions and (2) database + programming-fundamentals dominance in recurring keywords.
+          Recommendations below connect directly to these observed signals.
+        </p>
+        <div class="two">
+          <div>
+            <h3 style="margin: 10px 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">For teachers</h3>
+            <ul style="margin: 8px 0 0 18px;">
+              <li><strong>Align practice papers to the stable core:</strong> build drills that always include 15 MCQs and 3 Long questions, then vary Short count depending on target years.</li>
+              <li><strong>Prioritize database fundamentals:</strong> the strongest recurring signals are <em>database</em>, <em>table</em>, and <em>key</em> (e.g., “primary key”, “foreign key”).</li>
+              <li><strong>Reinforce programming mechanics:</strong> frequent vocabulary includes <em>function</em>, <em>loop</em>, <em>statement</em>, <em>operator</em>, and <em>printf</em>, suggesting repeated assessment of core C concepts.</li>
+              <li><strong>Short-question strategy:</strong> since total volume changes are explained by Short questions, prepare students for rapid, definition/difference-style responses (e.g., “format specifier”, “data type”).</li>
+            </ul>
+          </div>
+          <div>
+            <h3 style="margin: 10px 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">For curriculum planners</h3>
+            <ul style="margin: 8px 0 0 18px;">
+              <li><strong>Stability signal:</strong> repeated fixed counts (MCQs=15, Long=3) suggest a consistent blueprint in the observed data; review whether official policy mirrors this.</li>
+              <li><strong>Coverage check:</strong> database and programming tokens dominate frequency lists; if the curriculum intends broader breadth, ensure underrepresented topics are not being crowded out.</li>
+              <li><strong>Data-aware interpretation:</strong> dataset does not label annual vs supplementary papers; do not treat year-to-year changes as definitive policy shifts without cross-verification.</li>
+            </ul>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="card" id="conclusions">
+        <h2>Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>
+        <p>
+          Based strictly on <code class="inline">data.csv</code>, the BIEK Computer Science paper structure appears <strong>mostly stable</strong> across observed years, with variation concentrated in the number of Short questions.
+          Question content signals emphasize <strong>databases</strong> (keys, tables, relational model) and <strong>programming fundamentals</strong> (functions, loops, statements, operators, <code class="inline">printf</code>).
+        </p>
+        <h3 style="margin: 12px 0 6px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted);">Limitations (important for interpretation)</h3>
+        <ul style="margin: 8px 0 0 18px;">
+          <li><strong>Missing year:</strong> 2020 is absent in the dataset, so the 2019 → 2021 transition may be misleading if 2020 differs.</li>
